y  so  that's the sort of  overview of um this general approach for  self-supervised learning and now i will  dig into  the results and the application of this  idea in two different  settings in um in neuroscience and so  the first one is a slightly simpler task  or  a task where  an animal is trained to make reaching  movements  targets  there's um basically a cue that comes on  the screen and they'll make a reach so  standard center outreaching task we're  recording from neurons in the primary  motor cortex in this case  roughly about 100 to 200 neurons across  these different data sets and what i'm  showing here are  the results of essentially training this  neural network using this local  non-local  strategy in order to find  representations and then comparing with  other baselines  and  what we do is we  train the network in an unsupervised way  we basically freeze the the weights  and then the goal is to use the  representations to just form a linear  readout so only a single linear layer  that's appended to the representation to  to read out some um decoding information  so we're using the c coding task as a  way of understanding  how well  the reach direction in this case is  already being embedded within the the  neural uh the representations of the  neural activity so  um each group of bars is a different  data set we have two different days  from um two individuals so chewie and  mihi  and um comparing with an auto encoder  and simclear by also other um ssl  methods  and so  the take the take-home message here  would be that you know  not only does meow  the proposed approach that uses a  combination of local and non-local  augmentations you know provide a  significant improvement over these other  baseline approaches the other thing to  take away is that if we look at the  supervised performance so  um  the accuracy that we get when we test on  a held out chunk of time  on a supervised decoder trained on the  same task  what we find is that actually the ssl  approaches will often  outperform a supervised decoder which is  a really exciting result because it  shows us that there are  important forms of um regularization  and um  you know information that we're  embedding through these augmentations  that allow  um a neural network to perform better at  test time on held out data so we're  forming in some sense more stable  representations  that allow us to  decode this particular behavior  effectively  we can also  look at the  um the latent spaces or sort of  visualize the representations that are  being formed in this case and we find  consistently that  um by using our strategy we can preserve  more of the global structure of the task  so here showing results from meow  after applying um t-sne in this case to  find some low dimensional embeddings and  here we're finding that the kind of  overarc you know the the circular nature  of the task is being preserved within  the representation space whereas we  don't see that with some of the other  competing methods  um  here i'm just sort of showing  a number of the ablations that we  provide in the paper  which is out on archive  to kind of explore how  incorporating different sources of noise  or as i talked about before  um different amounts of dropout right  how how does that drop out or the  temporal jitter augmentations that i  described how does that impact the  overall performance in terms of first  building this stable and good  representation and what we find is that  consistently  um  both a combination of dropout and this  sort of local prediction that i  described is both of those things are  essential for building  meaningful  biases or regularization into the  network that allows us to then really  leverage this non-local structure  further with with meow  and these are just now kind of zooming  or looking at the representations of the  data in three dimensions and showing  that we can both preserve  the different reach directions which i'm  showing here over on the left where all  the different points are color coded  by the actual restriction we're showing  nice separability but in addition over  on the right i'm just showing that the  um the other aspect of this task which  you know is moving from a lower to high  high velocity in this outer portion of  the latent space  both of these aspects of the of the  behavior are being  captured in these in these neural  representations  formed by meow  okay  um so after establishing a method on a  number of different data sets in the um  in the macaque we then went on to apply  our approach to  free behavior so  um  in this case we have recordings from  different rodents i'll mainly talk about  well i have an example from the mouse  hippocampus on the right and also from  rat v1 and we're trying to expand this  into even more diverse brain regions and  really understand how well these sorts  of ideas generalize to different sorts  of behavior and different brain areas  um and so what i'm showing here now is  we apply  we apply approach and now we just read  out  through this linear layer we're going to  read out information about sleep state  so  here we're trying to decode rem from  non-rem from wake  across large 12-hour blocks of data so  this is while the animal is running  around in their cage and there's no  explicit  excuse me no explicit  training it's just free behavior  and so what we find or what we can you  know say is that drop out as an  augmentation  continues to be a powerful form of  regularization and we find that our  results overall hold in terms of you  know either outperforming or matching a  supervised decoder on this sleep task  where we only have three classes and so  it's a bit of an easier readout  and you know really just further  confirming that these ideas can be  applied in more diverse domains  what's interesting is that even though  our  behavioral labels are relatively coarse  right  being awake or being asleep um if we  actually look 