eping so a quiet wake state  um being more active and playful and  moving with moving their box and other  objects  and um and so on and so forth so we're  starting to now um be able to leverage  the  unique  insights provided by self-supervised  methods and our approach in particular  meow and use that with these really  large untethered data sets to build um  insights into encoding in this case in  in hippocampus  that we're really excited about uh  you know  describing in terms of the  neuroscientific implications as well  all right and before i end i'll just  kind of quickly give a plug for some  related work  um where we've you know been able to  take this idea  of um  invariance-based learning with  self-supervised learning through meow  and  kind of combine that with a generative  modeling approach or variational  autoencoder and this is recent work i  have references here if you're  interested  called swap vae which essentially is  combining um  these self-supervised methods for neural  activity  along with a vae to kind of hopefully  get the best of both where you can see  the things that are you know kind of  stable  and common  across local views  while also having a full range of  variability  necessary to actually generate realistic  neural activity  and we are using this to  understand and try to build disentangled  representations  of neural activity  all right so um i'll go ahead and  conclude so in summary i um described  this new approach for ssl called mind  your own view  it's  i didn't describe the results in natural  images but we're finding that we can  compete with state-of-the-art methods in  cfar and tiny imagenet and some of these  other computer vision data sets  um  we described specifically the  application of meow to neural population  activities discuss what it means to form  augmentations of neural activity and  show that those work in both rodan and  macaque  and then also demonstrating that a  cross-sample prediction with meow ofte